{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>His impurity through his flux.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said: ״His impurity through his flux״ — to exclude that he does not impart impurity through blood up to this point. That is, and apart from this exclusion, even though the verse says ״his flux — it is impure,״ and they expounded in Torat Kohanim that this comes to exclude blood that issues, [ruling] that [the zav] does not impart impurity [through it] — they expounded thus only because after the truth, the verse made a small exclusion that the zav does not impart impurity through blood. But if there had been no exclusion of the impurity of blood, then blood too would have been included in the general statement ״his flux — [it is] impure,״ since both are called ״his flux,״ and we would not have been able to exclude blood, and we would have been compelled to interpret the exclusion as referring to the impurity of the zav — that he does not impart impurity through blood — and we would have had no basis to exclude the issuing blood [of the zavah], that she does not impart impurity [through it], and it would have been derived by a kal va-chomer from his saliva, as stated there. Or we would have contrived to exclude it from what would be needed for the inclusion of ״וזאת,״ as we wrote above." "<b>It is his impurity.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded: And if the zav, who does not impart impurity through blood, does impart impurity through white [discharge] — then the zavah, who imparts impurity through blood, is it not logically certain that she imparts impurity through white [discharge]? Scripture says: ״It is his impurity.״ Meaning, it should not have needed to say ״his impurity,״ but it comes to exclude the zavah — ״his impurity״ and not her impurity. And it is not from the word ״הוא״ that [Scripture] excludes the zavah, according to the words of Ba'al Korban Aharon, for when we come to draw an inference from the implication of the exclusion that one infers from the word ״הוא,״ the exclusion would apply to the impurity itself and not to the person who is impure — and understand this." "And regarding what must be explained in this beraita — that it was necessary to exclude the zavah from imparting impurity through white [discharge], so that one not reason by kal va-chomer from the zav; whereas at the beginning of this beraita it excludes the zav from imparting impurity through blood, so that one not reason by kal va-chomer from the zavah — and these are two kal va-chomers that contradict each other — I have already elaborated in this parashah on the verse ״והזה על המטהר״ (Leviticus 14:7), and according to the approach I wrote there, their words, of blessed memory, will also be resolved in this place. For there is a stringency in the case of the nidah: her blood imparts impurity moist and dry (Niddah 54b), unlike the flux of the zav, which imparts impurity only when moist; and she imparts impurity to bed and seat in a single sighting, which the zav does not — and therefore there is grounds to magnify the impurity of blood. And there is a stringency in the zav: he requires no [specified] days but only sightings, and also counts seven for two sightings, which the zavah does not. If so, there are grounds for one [person] to magnify this stringency and to reason a kal va-chomer in this manner, and for another to magnify that stringency and to reason in that manner — and understand this." "And the reason for the verse's saying the word ״היא״ — it appears that the verse was concerned lest one say: the verse did not exclude white [discharge] from the zavah except to exclude it from the stringencies of blood, namely, to impart impurity to bed and seat in a single sighting — so as not to say, infer from it and apply the same in its [own] domain, but as for imparting impurity with the impurity of the zav, I still maintain that indeed she does. Therefore Scripture says a second exclusion, ״היא,״ meaning that the exclusion applies to the very existence of the impurity of the zav itself, which exists only in him. And the precise measure of the matter is as follows: This impurity, according to the law that pertains to it, namely, that they impart impurity with two or three sightings — applies only to the zav, as is evident from the verse's saying ״his impurity״ and not that of the zavah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase 'אחר האמת' appears to mean 'in accordance with the straightforward truth of the verse's language'; rendered hyper-literally as 'after the truth.'", 1 "In the word 'הוא' (masculine 'he/it'), the masculine form could be read as excluding the feminine zavah; the author, following Ba'al Korban Aharon, argues that the method of inference from this word operates on the category of impurity rather than on the gender of the impure person.", 2 "The phrase 'ובראש ברייתא זו' means 'at the beginning of this beraita,' referring back to the Torat Kohanim teaching cited in comment 1.", 3 "The word 'היא' (feminine 'she/it') here refers back to 'טומאה' (impurity), which is grammatically feminine; it does not refer to the zavah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:28.273929592Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}